[s0l0!-header][h1]Allocating Memory for Strings[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

When allocating memory for a string pointer, you may want to use string length rather than the sizeof operator for calculating bytes. 
Consider the following program:[code format="c" codeId="2126"]char str20[20];
char *str = NULL;

strcpy(str20, "12345");
str = [b]malloc(strlen(str20) + 1);[/b] 
strcpy(str, str20);
printf("%s", str); [/code]
This approach is better memory management because you aren’t allocating more space than is needed to a pointer. When using [b]strlen[/b] to determine the number of bytes needed for a string, be sure to include one extra byte for the NULL character '\0'.
A [b]char[/b] is always one byte, so there is no need to multiply the memory requirements by sizeof(char).
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

