[s0l0!-header][h1]Dynamic Arrays[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Many algorithms implement a [b]dynamic array[/b] because this allows the number of elements to grow as needed. 
Because elements are not allocated all at once, dynamic arrays typically use a structure to keep track of current array size, current capacity, and a pointer to the elements, as in the following program.[code format="c" codeId="2127"]typedef struct {
  int *elements;
  int size;
  int cap;
} dyn_array;

dyn_array arr;

/* initialize array */
arr.size = 0;
[b]arr.elements = calloc(1, sizeof(*arr.elements) );[/b]
[b]arr.cap = 1;[/b]  /* room for 1 element */ [/code]
To expand by more elements:[code format="c" codeId="2128"]arr.elements = [b]realloc[/b](arr.elements, (5 + arr.cap) * sizeof(*arr.elements));
if (arr.elements != NULL)
  [b]arr.cap += 5;[/b] /* increase capacity */ [/code]
Adding an element to the array increases its size:[code format="c" codeId="2129"]if (arr.size < arr.cap) {
  arr.elements[arr.size] = 50;
  [b]arr.size++;[/b]
} else {
  printf("Need to expand the array.");
}[/code]
The entire program is written in main() for demonstration purposes. To properly implement a dynamic array, sub-tasks should be broken down into functions such as init_array(), increase_array(), add_element(), and display_array(). The error checking was also skipped to keep the demo short.
